00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:06,300
While color images of space are incredibly gorgeous to look at, there are so many reasons

53
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:09,880
behind our efforts to visualize web data in color.

54
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:14,480
There are many wavelengths of light around us every day that we can actually see.

55
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,960
The web space telescope sees infrared light, which is a type of light that's invisible

56
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:19,040
to our eyes.

57
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:23,600
If you wore sophisticated infrared goggles and flew through space, you'd see the universe

58
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:25,440
similar to how web does.

59
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:29,160
But since our eyes can't naturally see an infrared, to see what web sees, we need to

60
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:31,560
translate the light into visible color.

61
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:36,600
Web captures multiple images using different filters that detect specific elements or molecules.

62
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:42,040
Then we combine those images to make a composite, assigning different colors to individual images

63
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:44,360
based on the wavelength of light they detect.

64
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:48,840
Just like you would translate a language you don't understand into one that you do, assigning

65
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:53,640
colors this way helps us process the information in a way our eyes can understand.

66
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:56,880
The words are always there, but now we can understand what they're saying.

67
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:02,080
After translating the infrared data to visible color, it's time to consider the aesthetics.

68
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:06,520
We use principles of photography to emphasize certain details and ensure that the image is

69
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:11,000
visually engaging while we work with scientists to ensure we're maintaining the integrity

70
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:12,960
of the original data.

71
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:15,000
Composition is also a big part of this process.

72
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,240
Sometimes this just means rotating an image to draw the eye to important info, and sometimes

73
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:22,360
this means cropping images to create a more balanced look.

74
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:27,080
There's no up in space, so our team gets to essentially decide what up looks like for

75
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,760
each image we release.

76
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:32,600
Composing color images from web's infrared data is a really unique combination of science

77
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:33,600
and art.

78
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:38,520
Similar to composing a symphony, we're carefully balancing each note or each color in scientific

79
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:44,000
detail to create a harmonious, scientifically meaningful, and visually stunning view of the universe.

80
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:54,600
Web has two main cameras, nearcam, a near infrared camera, and mirrory, the mid infrared instrument.

81
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:59,200
Nearcam captures shorter wavelengths of infrared light while mirrory captures longer wavelengths.

82
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:02,400
Nearcam has 29 filters while mirrory has 9 filters.

83
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:06,600
There are a large number of filter combinations that can appear in a web image.

84
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:11,240
We assign colors in chromatic order, blue, green, red, from shortest to longest wavelengths

85
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:13,600
for both of web's cameras.

86
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:17,640
We then carefully review what each image contains and decide which filters to keep in the final

87
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:22,000
composite image based on the data quality, science, and overall look.

88
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,440
We'll create images from each camera, but we'll often have the opportunity to combine

89
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:30,200
images from both cameras, which can produce stunning results.

90
